  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM MDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nNot much pattern change expected during the next week, as the H5\nridge remains firmly anchored on the Four Corners. This will\nkeep very warm temperatures in place across most of the state,\nwith highs each afternoon in the 90s. Today will be the warmest\nof these days, with some 100 degree readings for the Bighorn\nBasin and along the I-25 corridor. \n\nEven with the ridge holding hot temperatures in place, the\nplacement of the ridge will still allow monsoon moisture to draw\nnortheast into the western half of the state. This will bring\ndaily chances for showers and thunderstorms building along\nthe higher elevations of the western mountain ranges, then\ndrifting northeast around the ridge into Montana. Shower chances\nwill be enhanced by any small-scale shortwaves wrapping around\nthe ridge. One of these will sweep through this afternoon across\nNW WY. Combine this enhancement with higher temperatures today\nto bring some strong thunderstorms. With dewpoint depressions\napproaching 50 degrees, very gusty winds would be the main\nimpact, with wind gusts in excess of 50 mph possible. As these\nstorms are moving northeast at a reasonable rate, localized\nflooding concerns are decreased today. Daytime thunderstorm\nchances will continue through the week, building during the\nafternoon before decreasing at sunset.",
